${C_B}LOOKING${C_0}
  ${C_C}$PROG${C_0}                     full report: memory, ports, groups, top processes
  ${C_C}$PROG top${C_0} [N]              N biggest processes by RAM        (default 15)
  ${C_C}$PROG swap${C_0} [N]             N biggest swap hogs               (default 15)
  ${C_C}$PROG idle${C_0}                 processes doing nothing, or left behind by a closed shell
  ${C_C}$PROG projects${C_0}             what each project costs, and the ports it owns
  ${C_C}$PROG doctor${C_0}               is this WSL box configured to survive your work
  ${C_C}$PROG group${C_0} <name>         every process in one group
  ${C_C}$PROG ports${C_0}                everything listening
  ${C_C}$PROG port${C_0} <n>             who owns port n, its project dir, its tree
  ${C_C}$PROG tree${C_0} <pid>           process tree under a pid
  ${C_C}$PROG dash${C_0} [secs]          live interactive dashboard        (default 2)
  ${C_C}$PROG watch${C_0} [secs]         alias for dash

${C_B}STOPPING${C_0}
  ${C_C}$PROG kill port${C_0} <n>        stop whatever serves port n, plus its children
  ${C_C}$PROG kill pid${C_0} <n>         stop one process plus its children
  ${C_C}$PROG kill group${C_0} <name>    stop every process in a group
  ${C_C}$PROG kill project${C_0} <name>  stop everything running in one project

  Kill previews exactly what dies and how much it frees, then asks.
  It refuses to touch your own shell or the session you are typing in.

  ${C_DIM}-y${C_0}   skip the confirmation
  ${C_DIM}-9${C_0}   SIGKILL instead of SIGTERM (if it ignored the polite ask)
  ${C_DIM}-d${C_0}   dry run - show the victims, kill nothing

${C_B}DASHBOARD${C_0} ${C_DIM}($PROG dash)${C_0}
  ${C_C}$(dash_keyspan)${C_0} $(dash_viewlist)   ${C_C}tab${C_0} cycle
  ${C_C}up down${C_0} move   ${C_C}s${C_0} sort   ${C_C}/${C_0} filter   ${C_C}t${C_0} tree
  ${C_C}x${C_0} stop the selection (asks first)   ${C_C}X${C_0} same with SIGKILL
  ${C_C}p${C_0} pause   ${C_C}+ -${C_0} refresh rate   ${C_C}?${C_0} help   ${C_C}q${C_0} quit

  Colour and block glyphs turn themselves off when the output is not a
  terminal, when NO_COLOR is set, or when the locale is not UTF-8.
  DEVPS_ASCII=1 keeps the colour and draws with # and . instead.

${C_B}REDACTING${C_0}
  ${C_C}--redact${C_0}  mask secrets in the command lines shown - URL passwords and
            any KEY=VALUE whose key names a password, token, secret or
            api key. DEVPS_REDACT=1 does the same. Worth it before
            pasting output into an issue or a screenshot.

${C_B}GROUPS${C_0}
